WN.chunk(30,{"n":[[1501,"Say their name and age, name the people in their family or home, and talk about what makes them special",["Says their first name and how old they are","Names people who live with them or look after them","Shares one thing that is special about them"],[]],[1502,"Put clothes on in a sensible order (pants before trousers, socks before shoes, coat last), find the front and back using the label, and work a big button or a zip, asking for help with the tricky part only",["Puts socks on before shoes","Turns a T-shirt so the label is at the back","Does up a big button or starts a zip with a little help"],[]],[1503,"Talk about their likes and dislikes and things they can do by themselves (put on shoes, pour a drink), and notice that friends may like different things",["Names favourite toys, foods or games","Shows something they can do by themselves","Says 'I like... but my friend likes...'"],[]],[1504,"Notice what you are feeling and put a name to it — being able to label an emotion is the first step to understanding and managing it",["emotional literacy research","Fostering Emotional Literacy in Young Children (HeadStart.gov)","emotion vocabulary development 4-11 years (PMC)"],[]],[1505,"Know and use the vocabulary of self-reflection — self-awareness, reflect, pattern, trigger, assumption, impact, perspective, and notice — and understand that having precise words for these inner experiences makes them easier to understand and talk about",["Use words like 'reflect', 'pattern', and 'trigger' accurately when describing their own behaviour — e.g. 'I notice a pattern: I get frustrated when I rush'","Explain what 'self-awareness' means in their own words and give a personal example","Use the word 'perspective' correctly — e.g. 'From my perspective it felt unfair, but I can see theirs too'"],[]],[1506,"Understand that feelings and actions are separate — you can feel something strongly without having to act on it straight away",["emotion regulation development research","Effects of Age and Gender in Emotion Regulation (PMC 2020)","expressive control to emotion regulation (PMC)"],[]],[1507,"Name a few things they can already do well and a few they are still practising, see both as part of who they are, and choose one thing to practise without putting themselves down for not being good at it yet",["Names at least two things they are already good at","Names one thing they are still practising","Chooses a practice goal without putting themselves down"],[]],[1508,"Notice patterns in your own reactions — 'I tend to respond like this when I'm tired, left out, or put on the spot'",["middle childhood self-reliance in emotion regulation","7-year developmental shift in self-understanding research"],[]],[1509,"Notice and name good things, big and small, and the people behind them, and see how a habit of gratitude can lift mood, without pretending bad feelings away",["Names three good things from their day","Says who helped make a good thing happen","Explains that gratitude sits alongside other feelings"],[]],[1510,"Reflect on how your behaviour lands on others — consider not just what you intended but what the actual impact was on the other person",["Describe how something they said or did affected someone else — e.g. 'When I didn't include them, they looked upset'","Predict what might happen before acting — e.g. 'If I take the last one without asking, they'll feel it's unfair'","After a conflict, explain what they could have done differently and what effect that would have had"],[]],[1511,"Notice when your first reading of a social situation might be wrong — your assumptions about why someone acted a certain way are not always facts",["Pause before reacting to a classmate's behaviour and consider an alternative explanation — e.g. 'Maybe they bumped me by accident, not on purpose'","Describe a time they assumed the worst about someone's intention and later found out they were wrong","When told about an ambiguous social situation, suggest at least two possible reasons for the other person's behaviour instead of jumping to one conclusion"],[]],[1512,"Reflect on your own growth over time — the things that challenge you now are not fixed, and noticing how you have already changed builds genuine self-knowledge",["emotion vocabulary development continues to age 11 (PMC)","self-reflection importance in children research"],[]],[1513,"Identify personal values (honesty, fairness, family, achievement), reflect on where they come from, and use them to guide choices when under pressure",["Names three personal values","Explains a choice that reflects a value","Recognises when peers' values differ"],[]]],"r":[[278,"Noticing and naming feelings requires the basic vocabulary of self-awareness and reflection"],[279,"Naming what you are feeling is emotional comprehension monitoring — the universal habit of noticing what's happening inside applied to emotional experience"],[615,"Recognising that your patterns have changed over time requires first having noticed those patterns in the present"],[616,"Reflecting on your own growth requires first having developed honest self-awareness of your assumptions and reactions — growth reflection is hollow without prior self-scrutiny"],[617,"Reflecting on your own personal growth is the PSD form of the universal learning-reflection habit"],[1082,"Noticing own patterns requires vocabulary of 'pattern', 'trigger', and 'reflect'"],[1083,"Noticing patterns in your reactions requires first understanding that feelings and responses are separable — you can only track a pattern once you're aware of the gap between feeling and action"],[1084,"Noticing recurring patterns in your own reactions is the PSD form of the universal pattern-recognition habit"],[1530,"Questioning own assumptions requires precise vocabulary of 'assumption', 'bias', and 'perspective'"],[1531,"Noticing that your first read of a situation might be wrong requires awareness of your own patterns of assumption and reaction"],[1532,"Questioning your assumptions about social situations requires first having practised the harder skill of seeing yourself from another person's perspective"],[1533,"Questioning your assumptions about why someone acted a certain way is elaborative interrogation applied to social cognition — asking 'why do I think this?' rather than accepting the first explanation"],[2016,"Reflecting on how your behaviour landed on others requires being able to articulate your own thinking and intentions clearly — the self-explanation habit applied to social experience"],[2017,"Reflecting on impact on others requires vocabulary of 'impact', 'perspective', and 'reflect'"],[2018,"Reflecting on the impact of your behaviour on others requires first having noticed patterns in your own reactions — you need self-knowledge before you can examine your social footprint"],[2019,"Reflecting on impact requires understanding that your actions were choices, not automatic responses to feelings — the feelings/actions distinction underpins social accountability"],[2189,"Understanding that feelings and actions are separate requires first being able to name and identify what you are feeling"],[2190,"Understanding the feelings-actions separation requires vocabulary to distinguish and name each component"],[4829,"Talking about likes and skills builds on knowing who they are"],[4971,"Values are part of identity and belonging"],[4972,"Reflecting on values draws on personal growth over time"],[5010,"Noticing what is good in life builds on talking about what they like"],[5011,"Gratitude is a partner habit to positive self-talk"],[5020,"Knowing body parts helps match each piece of clothing to where it goes"],[5021,"Dressing in order is a routine of steps that come one after another"],[5088,"Talking about what they can already do is the start of also naming what they are practising"],[5089,"A thing they are practising is one they can get better at"],[5090,"Words for strengths and practice come from the vocabulary they already use about themselves"],[5165,"Vocabulary about self builds on talking about me and my family"]],"c":{"Personal & Social Development|Self-Awareness|2":"Your child is learning to talk about themselves — their name, age and the people in their family.","Personal & Social Development|Self-Awareness|3":"Your child is talking about what they like and what they can now do by themselves, such as getting dressed in the right order.","Personal & Social Development|Self-Awareness|6":"Your child is learning to name things they are already good at and things they are still practising, without putting themselves down.","Personal & Social Development|Self-Awareness|8":"Your child is learning to notice good things and the people behind them (gratitude).","Personal & Social Development|Self-Awareness|14":"Your child is identifying their personal values and using them to guide choices under pressure."}});
